From: Ahrion Gallegos <ahrionmgallegos@gmail.com>
Subject: net: xdp: do not assume an Ethernet header when rebuilding an skb

__xdp_build_skb_from_frame() finishes with

	/* Essential SKB info: protocol and skb->dev */
	skb->protocol = eth_type_trans(skb, dev);

unconditionally. That is right only when the ingress device actually has
an Ethernet header in front of the payload, and there is nothing in the
function that checks. An xdp_frame carries no link-layer information of
its own, so the only thing that can answer the question is the device,
and the device is never asked.

On a raw-IP device the result is silent and total. eth_type_trans() reads
the IP version nibble as the first octet of a destination MAC. IPv4's
0x45 has the multicast bit set, so the frame comes out PACKET_MULTICAST;
IPv6's 0x60 does not, so it comes out PACKET_OTHERHOST. Neither is
PACKET_HOST, and ip_forward() rejects anything that is not, at
net/ipv4/ip_forward.c:93, under a comment reading "that should never
happen". Every forwarded datagram is dropped.

Where it bites: kernel/bpf/cpumap.c:348 calls this with xdpf->dev_rx, the
*ingress* device, so an XDP_REDIRECT into a cpumap from any raw-IP netdev
loses the traffic. drivers/net/veth.c:727 is the other caller and is
unaffected, being ARPHRD_ETHER; so are the driver-side users of the
xdp_build_skb_from_frame() wrapper. For every existing caller in the
Ethernet case this change is a no-op.

Worth stating because it explains why nobody has hit it: this is the
native path only. The generic path tags skbs into the same ptr_ring with
__ptr_test_bit(0, &ptr) (cpumap.c:127 and :313) and never rebuilds them,
so a device with only generic XDP cannot reach the bug. It becomes
reachable the moment a raw-IP driver gains native XDP -- and cpumap
redirect is one of the main reasons such a driver would want it, since it
is how per-packet work moves off the single CPU the modem's RX path runs
on. So the first thing that would be tried is the first thing that would
fail.

The fix asks the device and, when it is not Ethernet, does the same work
eth_type_trans() does minus the header it does not have.

Three details are load-bearing rather than stylistic:

  * skb_reset_mac_header() is required, not decorative.
    build_skb_around() above does not set mac_header, eth_type_trans()
    would have, and __netif_receive_skb_core() derives mac_len from it.
    Leaving it unset gives a garbage mac_len on a device where it should
    be 0.
  * pkt_type is set explicitly. On the Ethernet path it is derived from
    comparing the destination MAC; with no MAC to compare there is
    nothing to derive it from, and PACKET_HOST is correct by
    construction for a device that receives only its own traffic.
  * There is no pull. eth_type_trans() removes ETH_HLEN because it
    consumed a header; here there is no header to consume, and pulling
    would eat the first 14 bytes of the IP packet.

xdp_l3_protocol() is the same derivation the raw-IP drivers already carry
for the packets they build themselves -- mhi_wwan_mbim.c:355,
rmnet_handlers.c:22 and iosm_ipc_wwan.c:233 each have their own copy --
so a frame round-tripped through XDP is labelled the way one that was not
would be. It returns 0 for anything that is neither IPv4 nor IPv6, so an
undecodable payload finds no ptype handler and is dropped rather than
guessed at.

Not board-specific and not MediaTek-specific: it affects every
ARPHRD_NONE and ARPHRD_RAWIP netdev on any driver, which is every WWAN
modem in tree. It is a precondition for the driver-side native XDP patch
rather than a consequence of it: that patch delivers nothing through
cpumap until this is fixed as well.

Not an independent discovery. Alexander Lobakin named this exact failure
on netdev on 2026-08-18, reviewing Jiayuan Chen's "net: xdp: don't assume
an Ethernet header in generic XDP":

  Most XDP programs expect Ethernet header at the beginning of a frame.
  Unless you write a custom one which doesn't. But then you may face
  that XDP_TX, XDP_REDIRECT won't work properly -- cpumap Rx, each
  .ndo_xdp_xmit() implementation -- all expect Ethernet header at the
  beginning.

"cpumap Rx" is this patch. So the failure was described before I found
it, which is worth saying for attribution. It does not change what the
code does: the drop is real, reachable, and silent, and the fix is a
no-op for every Ethernet caller.

That thread was refused -- pw-bot: cr, no v2 -- and anyone sending this
upstream should know that. It is a different patch, though: the native
path rather than the generic one, a different function in a different
file, and it does not ask the core to relax an assumption on behalf of a
device class with no native XDP, because the driver patch this sits
under supplies one.

Evidence: E2 throughout, read at v6.12.103. net/core/xdp.c and
kernel/bpf/cpumap.c are pristine in this tree -- no patch here or in
OpenWrt's generic set touches either -- so the context is upstream's.
Verified to apply at --fuzz=0 with 870 through 872 already applied.
Shares no file with any of them.

Out-of-tree patch for the GL-X3000 lean build.

--- a/net/core/xdp.c
+++ b/net/core/xdp.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include <linux/idr.h>
 #include <linux/rhashtable.h>
 #include <linux/bug.h>
+#include <linux/if_arp.h>
 #include <net/page_pool/helpers.h>
 
 #include <net/hotdata.h>
@@ -650,6 +651,31 @@ int xdp_alloc_skb_bulk(void **skbs, int
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(xdp_alloc_skb_bulk);
 
+/* Derive the L3 protocol of a raw-IP frame from its version nibble.
+ *
+ * Matches what the raw-IP drivers already do for the packets they build
+ * themselves -- mhi_wwan_mbim.c, rmnet_handlers.c and iosm_ipc_wwan.c each
+ * carry their own copy of this -- so a frame that has been round-tripped
+ * through XDP arrives labelled the same way one that has not.
+ *
+ * 0 for anything else: an undecodable payload should find no ptype handler
+ * and be dropped, rather than be guessed at.
+ */
+static __be16 xdp_l3_protocol(const struct sk_buff *skb)
+{
+	if (unlikely(!skb->len))
+		return 0;
+
+	switch (skb->data[0] & 0xf0) {
+	case 0x40:
+		return htons(ETH_P_IP);
+	case 0x60:
+		return htons(ETH_P_IPV6);
+	default:
+		return 0;
+	}
+}
+
 struct sk_buff *__xdp_build_skb_from_frame(struct xdp_frame *xdpf,
 					   struct sk_buff *skb,
 					   struct net_device *dev)
@@ -687,8 +713,36 @@ struct sk_buff *__xdp_build_skb_from_fra
 					   nr_frags * xdpf->frame_sz,
 					   xdp_frame_is_frag_pfmemalloc(xdpf));
 
-	/* Essential SKB info: protocol and skb->dev */
-	skb->protocol = eth_type_trans(skb, dev);
+	/* Essential SKB info: protocol and skb->dev.
+	 *
+	 * eth_type_trans() is right only when the ingress device actually has
+	 * an Ethernet header in front of the payload. An xdp_frame carries no
+	 * link-layer information of its own, so the only way to know is to ask
+	 * the device.
+	 *
+	 * On a raw-IP device -- every WWAN modem netdev, among others --
+	 * running eth_type_trans() here reads the IP version nibble as the
+	 * first octet of a destination MAC. IPv4's 0x45 has the multicast bit
+	 * set, so the frame comes out PACKET_MULTICAST; IPv6's 0x60 does not,
+	 * so it comes out PACKET_OTHERHOST. Neither is PACKET_HOST, and
+	 * ip_forward() drops anything that is not, under a comment reading
+	 * "that should never happen".
+	 */
+	if (dev->type == ARPHRD_ETHER) {
+		skb->protocol = eth_type_trans(skb, dev);
+	} else {
+		/* The same work eth_type_trans() does, minus the header it does
+		 * not have: no pull, and no destination-MAC comparison to
+		 * derive pkt_type from, so it is PACKET_HOST by construction.
+		 * mac_header must be set because build_skb_around() above left
+		 * it unset and __netif_receive_skb_core() derives mac_len from
+		 * it.
+		 */
+		skb->dev = dev;
+		skb_reset_mac_header(skb);
+		skb->pkt_type = PACKET_HOST;
+		skb->protocol = xdp_l3_protocol(skb);
+	}
 
 	/* Optional SKB info, currently missing:
 	 * - HW checksum info		(skb->ip_summed)
